.TH ORO-AUTHENTICATION 3 "" "Oro Runtime" "Oro Runtime API Manual"
.SH NAME
oro:authentication \- JavaScript API module
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
import * as module from 'oro:authentication'
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Authentication\-gated Android 11+ Keystore and Linux hardware PKCS#11 operations. Enable `[permissions] allow_biometrics = true`. Linux supports explicit `biometric\-or\-device` policy through a provider PIN or protected device path; this does not attest biometrics or use the desktop login password. Defaults remain biometric\-only and reject on Linux. Account authority still requires server verification. See the Linux native integration guide for provider setup. iOS/iPadOS, macOS and Windows backends for this API are not implemented. `getStatus()` reports unavailable with platform/implementation/reason metadata; operational calls reject ENOSYS. Hardware, enrollment and provider\-policy failures are separate admission errors and must not be reported as success.
.SH REFERENCE
.TP
\fBgetStatus(options)\fR
Reads policy/enrollment availability without prompting or creating keys. Missing or platform\-restricted backends return unavailable status; operational calls still reject with ENOSYS.
\fBParameters:\fR
.br
\fIoptions\fR (AuthenticationOptions; optional)
\fBReturns:\fR
.br
\fIPromise<AuthenticationStatus>\fR
.TP
\fBseal(value, options)\fR
Encrypts at most 64 KiB after a native cryptographic authentication prompt. At most 64 protected keys are retained. Existing keys are never silently replaced after enrollment changes, removal or invalidation. Keep the returned sealed record and handle EKEYREVOKED/ENOENT through explicit app recovery.
\fBParameters:\fR
.br
\fIvalue\fR (string \\| Uint8Array \\| ArrayBuffer; required)
.br
\fIoptions\fR (ProtectedKeyOptions; required)
\fBReturns:\fR
.br
\fIPromise<ProtectedSecret>\fR
.TP
\fBopen(sealed, options)\fR
Decrypts through an Android authenticated Cipher or Linux authenticated hardware unwrap followed by native AES\-GCM. Linux ephemeral AES keys exist briefly in native memory and are erased after use; long\-lived private keys stay in the provider. Returns bytes; application session recovery remains app\-owned.
\fBParameters:\fR
.br
\fIsealed\fR (ProtectedSecret; required)
.br
\fIoptions\fR (ProtectedKeyOptions; required)
\fBReturns:\fR
.br
\fIPromise<Uint8Array>\fR
.TP
\fBcreateSigningKey(options)\fR
Creates or reuses a non\-exportable P\-256 key and signs an enrollment challenge after cryptographic authentication. This does not enroll the key on a server.
\fBParameters:\fR
.br
\fIoptions\fR (ChallengeOptions; required)
\fBReturns:\fR
.br
\fIPromise<ChallengeSignature>\fR
.TP
\fBsignChallenge(options)\fR
Signs a fresh authority/account\-bound server challenge using an existing authentication\-gated key. No success boolean establishes server authority.
\fBParameters:\fR
.br
\fIoptions\fR (ChallengeOptions; required)
\fBReturns:\fR
.br
\fIPromise<ChallengeSignature>\fR
.TP
\fBremoveKey(options)\fR
Explicitly removes this app's protected secret or signing key. Ciphertext and server enrollment become unusable; revoke enrolled keys on the server too.
\fBParameters:\fR
.br
\fIoptions\fR (ProtectedKeyRemovalOptions; required)
\fBReturns:\fR
.br
\fIPromise<void>\fR
